[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Did you know that you can create clean and professional looking motion graphics using AI? And you can do it without ever opening After Effects, [music] as long as you know how to properly guide the AI. In this video, I\'ll show how to do that step-by-step by building motion graphics from scratch, [music] starting'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from a simple title card all the way up to a full animated interface. I will start with the most basic motion graphic anyone can create, which is an animated [music] title card. To generate everything in this video, I\'ll use a'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'platform called OpenArt. If you don\'t know about it, it has all the best AI models in one place, [music] and it helps people create AI-generated content seamlessly. I\'ll leave a link in the description if you want to follow along.'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I will leave a prompt asset in the description too if you want to use the ones I did. So, let\'s begin. First, I\'ll click image to open the create image workspace. I\'ll set the image generation model to Nano Banana Pro. I\'m using Nano'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Banana Pro here because it\'s Google\'s best model for clean, high-quality graphics. [music] And a simple title card like this doesn\'t need anything more. I\'ll switch to GPT Image 2 later for the interfaces, where there\'s a lot of small text like'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prices and buttons that has to come out exactly right. [music] I\'ll set the aspect ratio to 16x9, the resolution to 4K, and leave auto polish switched off by the prompt box so that it doesn\'t alter my prompt. Then I\'ll paste in this'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt to generate our [music] first frame. And what I got is an off-white blank canvas. Next, I\'ll upload that blank canvas as the reference image and paste in this prompt second frame and hit generate. [music] And I got a clean'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'text image overlaying the off-white background. Next, I\'ll head over to video, open frame to video, and switch the model to C Dance 2.0. I\'ll load the off-white blank canvas as the start frame and the text image as the end'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'frame. Then I\'ll set the duration to 4 seconds, the resolution to 1080p, and leave the audio off since this motion graphic doesn\'t need audio. Now, I\'ll paste in the prompt and hit generate. [music] As you can see, we\'ve got a'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clean, simple animation for the text with the blue stripe underneath animating as well. Overall, I really like it. So, why did I go through all that instead of just dropping in one image and telling the model to animate the text? Well, because that is where it'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mostly goes wrong. A video model doesn\'t move your design the way Adobe After Effects does. It generates [music] every single frame of the video from scratch. And because it\'s redrawing the whole frame every time, there\'s nothing'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keeping your text in place. So, there\'s a high possibility your text will change and warp through the whole video. And at the end, it will not look good at all. But by giving it a start frame [music] and an end frame, the AI doesn\'t have to'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'invent how the motion graphic should start or end. It only fills in the movement between the two. So, it\'s a good starting place for the AI and it minimizes our chance of error. So, with a simple process, we\'ve got our first'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'animated motion graphic. But two frames only get you so far. Next, we\'ll move on to an elevated version of this. So, this time I\'ll make an advanced title card [music] instead of a basic one. I\'ll stay in the create image workspace with'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Nano Banana Pro as the model [music] and keep the settings the same as before. First, I\'ll paste in this prompt and hit generate. And what I got is a pitch-black canvas I\'ll use as the start frame. Next, [music] I\'ll upload that'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exact frame as the reference image, paste in this prompt, and generate. [music] In this image, the number one is centered in the middle of the frame with some ambient glow behind it and a 3D motion graphic feel to it. Next, I\'ll'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'upload that second frame as the reference, paste in our third prompt, and generate. In this one, I\'ve got some text just beside our number. And if you noticed, the placement of the number is different from the last frame. [music] Next, I\'ll head over to video, open text'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to video, and switch the model to SeeDance 2.0 again. I\'ll load the blank canvas as the start frame and the number with the headline as the end [music] frame. Then I\'ll add in the middle frame as a reference image. I\'ll set the'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'duration to 6 seconds, the resolution to 1080p, [music] and leave the audio off since this motion graphic doesn\'t need it either. I\'ll paste in the prompt and generate. As you can see, the animation I got is very fluid and satisfying to'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look at. And if we just increase the playback speed, this will be perfect to use in a video. Because I uploaded three images, the blank [music] canvas as the start frame and the number and headline as the middle and end frame, I guided'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the AI on how it should animate. And that\'s how we got such a good result. Using this exact formula, you can a ton of [music] different variations of this exact motion graphic for yourself. But these are still just numbers and text on'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the screen. So this time, instead of just a text animation, I\'ll make an actual interface to showcase how it reacts when you click through it. I\'ll head back into the create image workspace, but this time I\'ll switch the'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model to GPT image 2 because it\'s a lot better at generating clean interfaces and readable text. Next, [music] I\'ll set the quality to high, the aspect ratio to 16 by 9, and the resolution to 4K. First, I\'ll paste in this prompt and'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hit generate. [music] I\'ve got a really simple off-white blank canvas as our first image. Next, I\'ll paste in this prompt and hit generate. What I got is a product card for our interface, and it has three different'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dimensions to it. There\'s the plain background, the interface card on top of it, and the product image card inside it. Next, I\'ll upload that exact image as the reference and paste [music] in this prompt, then hit generate. In this'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image, the empty space in our interface card is filled in with the text, buy button, and the price. For our last frame, I\'ll upload the blank canvas I generated earlier as the reference and paste in the [music] prompt, then hit'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generate. With that, I got a really simple ending frame. To move forward, I\'ll click video from the sidebar and open the video workspace. I\'ll [music] switch to text to video and set the model to seedance 2.0. I\'ll load in the blank canvas as the start frame and set'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the confirmation screen as the end frame. I\'ll set the duration to 8 seconds, the resolution [music] to 1080p, and this time I\'ll leave the audio off. Then I\'ll hit generate. As you can see, the animation is really'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fluid. The cursor clicks through the right buttons, and overall the interface [music] reacted very accurately. It\'s very usable, but now what I want is a version of this with audio attached. So, for that I\'ll paste in a slightly upgraded prompt [music] for better sound'

[06:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'design and toggle the audio switch on. The rest of the settings stay the same, and I\'ll hit generate. Compared to the previous one, this video'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has a really interactive and engaging audio to it. And the cursor even makes a click sound when it interacts. Overall, I think it\'s really good. And you can control how you want the audio to sound just by [music] customizing the prompt.'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And you can use this to create any version of an interface motion [music] graphic you want, even with sounds and other elements, too. But this is still a very short interaction, and it has just one button and one click in the whole'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scene. Next, I\'ll take this all the way to a multi-step motion graphic. So, let\'s take everything we have learned and create one animation out of it. I\'ll head back to create image with GPT image 2 as [music] the image generation model,'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and I\'ll keep the rest of the settings the same. First, I\'ll paste in this prompt and hit generate. [music] Now, this scene\'s a lot bigger than the last ones. So, instead of two or three frames, I\'ll use six. Each image will be assigned to one step at a time. So,'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first I\'ll upload my previous image as the reference and paste in this [music] prompt. What I got is a simple search interface. Then I\'ll upload that as a reference for the [music] next image, paste in this prompt, and hit generate.'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This shows the sort menu opening up. Doing the same as before, I\'ll generate an image that shows picking one of the filters. And after that, I\'ll generate another one showing the product coming up with its order button. And the'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] last one\'s just the order confirmation. Next, I\'ll head over to video, open text to video, and switch [music] to C Dance 2.0. I\'ll drop the search page in as the start frame and the confirmation as the end. Then I\'ll add the four middle frames as'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'references, so it knows every step to hit [music] on the way. I\'ll set it to 15 seconds, 1080p, and leave the audio on for the sound design. Then I\'ll paste in the prompt and hit generate. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; And this is where it all comes together. Overall, it\'s a very fluid and nice-looking [music] animation with multiple steps executed properly. By combining all of our images, the AI model picked out exactly what I meant'

[08:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and implemented the instructions where it needed to be. And as a result, we got this animation. [music] And this is honestly the exact same method from our very first title card. It\'s just a little bigger. The start and'

[08:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'end frames [music] lock down where the video begins and ends, and the four images in the middle keep everything in between right in their place. [music] That\'s how a 15-second scene with this much going on comes out as one clean video instead of [music] a dozen'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'separate ones I\'d have to stitch together. And since I left the audio on, it even adds the typing sound, [music] the clicks, and the confirmation sound, all matching what\'s on screen. And that\'s how you go from creating a simple title screen to generating a complex'

[09:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multi-step motion graphic. Now, let\'s create something that I can use regularly across my content. For this [music] one, I\'ll create a couple of dark mode animations. First for a subscribe button and next [music] for a'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'follow button. I\'ll head back into create image and set the model to GPT Image 2. I\'ll push the quality to high, then paste in this prompt, and [music] hit generate. Next, I\'ll upload that background along with a real photo of'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'myself and paste [music] in the next prompt. As you can see, I\'ve created a subscribe card on top of the blank background. Then I\'ll upload that same image again and paste in this prompt. Now, the subscribe button looks like it\'s already been [music] used and the'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'channel is subscribed. Now, I\'ll head over to video, open text to video, and switch to C-Dance 2.0. [music] I\'ll load the blank background as the start frame and the subscribe one as the end frame. Then I\'ll add the middle frame as a'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference, along with my photo again. I\'ll set the duration to 8 seconds, bump the resolution up to 4K, and leave the audio on. I\'ll paste in the prompt and hit generate. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; In the video, I\'ve got a really nice motion graphic showing [music] the gestures to subscribe to my channel. Overall, the animation came out very clear and it\'s instantly [music] usable in any of my videos. Next, I\'ll create another animation. This one built for'

[10:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'following on Instagram. I\'ll head back into create image and set the model to GPT [music] image 2. I\'ll push the quality to high, then paste in this prompt and hit generate. Next, I\'ll upload that background along with a real photo of myself and paste in the [music]'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next prompt. As you can see, I\'ve created a follow card on top of the blank background. Again, just like before, I\'ll upload that generated image and paste in this prompt. And I got another one showing that my profile is'

[10:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'already followed. To move forward, I\'ll head back into C-Dance and upload my start and end frames. Then I\'ll [music] paste in the prompt and hit generate. This one\'s also a really good animation,'

[11:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just like the previous one. But if you notice, when my head pops up in the video, my hair and face also have a little bit of animation, which makes it feel a little more alive. And the sound effects [music] are spot on, too. Using'

[11:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this exact workflow, you can make these kinds of motion graphics for any [music] platform you want. As you have seen so far, we have not used After Effects to make a single motion graphic in this video. I have made all of them using'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just a single platform, and it\'s Open Art. So, go sign up to the Open Art using my description below [music] and start creating motion graphics for yourself. Thanks for watching. I\'ll see you in the next one.'
